WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.430
We left a soil sensor in the school

2
00:00:02.430 --> 00:00:03.950
garden over the long holiday.

3
00:00:04.710 --> 00:00:07.490
It sent a reading over Wi-Fi every

4
00:00:07.490 --> 00:00:09.810
minute, on two AA batteries.

5
00:00:10.550 --> 00:00:12.250
It died in under three weeks.

6
00:00:13.070 --> 00:00:14.950
The holiday lasted eight.

7
00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:19.120
Today: power from batteries and the sun.

8
00:00:20.410 --> 00:00:22.210
A battery's capacity is in

9
00:00:22.210 --> 00:00:25.010
milliamp-hours: two AA batteries

10
00:00:25.010 --> 00:00:26.890
hold about two thousand five hundred.

11
00:00:27.510 --> 00:00:29.410
Divide it by the average current,

12
00:00:29.770 --> 00:00:31.950
in milliamps, to get hours.

13
00:00:32.790 --> 00:00:34.250
And plan with seventy

14
00:00:34.250 --> 00:00:35.430
percent of the label.

15
00:00:36.670 --> 00:00:38.310
Where does the power go?

16
00:00:38.950 --> 00:00:41.770
Asleep, a sensor draws about a hundredth

17
00:00:41.770 --> 00:00:44.090
of a milliamp; measuring takes about

18
00:00:44.090 --> 00:00:45.890
five, for a tenth of a second.

19
00:00:46.540 --> 00:00:49.080
But sending over Wi-Fi takes a hundred

20
00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:50.900
and twenty, for two whole seconds.

21
00:00:52.250 --> 00:00:55.210
So our garden sensor, sending once a

22
00:00:55.210 --> 00:00:57.770
minute, averaged about four milliamps.

23
00:00:58.450 --> 00:01:00.470
Seventy percent of two thousand

24
00:01:00.470 --> 00:01:02.610
five hundred is one thousand

25
00:01:02.610 --> 00:01:03.590
seven hundred and fifty.

26
00:01:04.270 --> 00:01:06.990
Divided by four, that's about four

27
00:01:06.990 --> 00:01:09.310
hundred and forty hours: eighteen days.

28
00:01:10.570 --> 00:01:11.150
Quick check!

29
00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:14.860
Which change makes the batteries last

30
00:01:14.860 --> 00:01:18.260
longest: measuring less often,

31
00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:22.460
sending every fifteen minutes, or

32
00:01:22.460 --> 00:01:24.620
four batteries instead of two?

33
00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:27.620
Send every fifteen minutes.

34
00:01:28.350 --> 00:01:30.130
The average drops from four

35
00:01:30.130 --> 00:01:32.290
milliamps to under a third of one,

36
00:01:32.750 --> 00:01:34.890
so the same batteries last about

37
00:01:34.890 --> 00:01:37.030
nine months, not eighteen days.

38
00:01:37.720 --> 00:01:39.860
Four batteries would only double it.

39
00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:43.900
To run all year, add a small solar

40
00:01:43.900 --> 00:01:45.720
panel and a rechargeable battery.

41
00:01:46.370 --> 00:01:48.970
The panel charges by day; the battery

42
00:01:48.970 --> 00:01:51.290
carries the sensor through nights and

43
00:01:51.290 --> 00:01:51.590
clouds.

44
00:01:52.390 --> 00:01:54.530
Much of Africa and the Middle East gets

45
00:01:54.530 --> 00:01:57.330
four to six hours of full sun a day, but

46
00:01:57.330 --> 00:01:59.710
plan for three to five cloudy days in a

47
00:01:59.710 --> 00:01:59.890
row.

48
00:02:01.090 --> 00:02:02.950
Size it for our Wi-Fi sensor.

49
00:02:03.610 --> 00:02:06.410
It uses about a hundred milliamp-hours

50
00:02:06.410 --> 00:02:09.530
a day; five cloudy days need five

51
00:02:09.530 --> 00:02:12.430
hundred, or a little over seven hundred

52
00:02:12.430 --> 00:02:13.850
with the seventy percent rule.

53
00:02:14.410 --> 00:02:16.650
And the panel should make three times the

54
00:02:16.650 --> 00:02:18.770
daily use, to catch up after clouds.

55
00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:20.840
Your turn.

56
00:02:21.460 --> 00:02:24.080
This battery covers five cloudy

57
00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:26.500
days, but the panel only just

58
00:02:26.500 --> 00:02:28.880
covers one day's use in full sun.

59
00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:31.080
Will it run all year?

60
00:02:32.290 --> 00:02:35.350
No: with nothing to spare, the panel

61
00:02:35.350 --> 00:02:37.170
can't catch up after cloudy days.

62
00:02:37.910 --> 00:02:41.150
Remember: capacity divided by average

63
00:02:41.150 --> 00:02:44.510
current gives hours, plan with seventy

64
00:02:44.510 --> 00:02:47.650
percent, send less often, and give the

65
00:02:47.650 --> 00:02:48.930
panel room to spare.

66
00:02:50.260 --> 00:02:51.620
Try it in class tomorrow:

67
00:02:51.860 --> 00:02:53.520
battery-life sums on the board.

68
00:02:54.140 --> 00:02:56.800
Give each group a sensor card with a

69
00:02:56.800 --> 00:02:59.680
battery, a radio and how often it sends.

70
00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:02.800
They work out how long it lasts, then

71
00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:05.360
change one thing to make it last a term.

72
00:03:05.990 --> 00:03:08.750
Next time: getting the data home.
